<section class="panel" aria-label="Flexible addition and algorithms source-grounded lesson">
  <h2>Warm-up — Number Talk</h2>
  <p>Find mentally, one expression at a time; explain how a close hundred helps you adjust accurately.</p>
  <ol><li>300 + 156</li><li>299 + 156</li><li>303 + 156</li><li>204 + 376</li></ol>
  <h2>Activity 1 — Just Ones</h2>
  <p>Two records of 657 + 286 show different ways to write newly composed units. These are independent text illustrations; the original diagrams remain with the publisher.</p>
  <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:12px">
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Recording method 1</h3><p>Ones: 7 + 6 = 13, newly composed ten = 10.</p><p>Tens: 50 + 80 + 10 = 140, newly composed hundred = 100.</p><p>Hundreds: 600 + 200 + 100 = 900.</p><p>943.</p></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Recording method 2</h3><p>Write 1 above the tens column to mean 10; write 1 above the hundreds column to mean 100.</p><p>Three ones, four tens and nine hundreds.</p><p>943.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>With a partner, explain why the carried digit 1 does not have the same value when placed in different columns. Use MLR8 speaking supports. Try the second method for 602+179, 493+161, 438+364 and 329+381.</p>
  <h2>Activity 2 — How Would You Add?</h2>
  <p>Choose a strategy or an algorithm for each sum and organize your reasoning so others can follow. First compare with a partner using the same method, then with a partner using a different method.</p>
  <ol><li>199 + 348</li><li>264 + 359</li><li>203 + 75</li><li>316 + 198</li><li>399 + 499</li></ol>
  <p>Discuss which sums have friendly-hundred adjustments, for instance 399 + 499 = 400 + 500 − 2. The teacher evaluates the choice and clarity of each method, not a numeric-only answer.</p>
  <h2>Synthesis — Choosing an Approach</h2>
  <p>Explain when you prefer a reliable algorithm and when a flexible strategy is quicker. The original 2021 Student Task Statements PDF and third-party photographs are not reproduced or artistically certified; optional original gameboards and the gated publisher Cool-down require teacher access.</p>
